1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiao Yuansu 蕭圓肅 (539–584), ''zi'' Minggong 明恭. Northern Zhou scholar and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Yuansu's ancestral home was Nan Lanling 南蘭陵 (northwest of modern Changzhou 常州, Jiangsu). He was the grandson of Xiao Yan 蕭衍 (464–549), Emperor Wu (r. 502–549) of the Liang dynasty, and the son of Xiao Ji 蕭紀 (508–553), Prince 
2 CONC of Wuling 武陵, the eighth son of Xiao Yan. He had an elegant demeanor and was a diligent student. In May–June 552, during the Hou Jing 侯景 rebellion of 548–552, Xiao Ji installed himself as emperor in Shu 蜀 (modern Sichuan). He name
2 CONC d Xiao Yuansu Prince of Yidu 宜都 and appointed him palace attendant and General Who Pacifies the Distance. He ordered Xiao Hui 蕭撝 (515–573), director of the Imperial Secretariat and regional inspector of Yizhou 益州 (administrative s
2 CONC eat in modern Chengdu, Sichuan), to defend Chengdu. He assigned Xiao Yuansu as Xiao Hui's assistant. In 553, the Western Wei general Yuchi Jiong 尉遲迥 (d. 580) led an assault on Chengdu. Xiao Hui and Xiao Yuansu did battle for fifty
2 CONC  days without receiving supplies or provisions. They finally surrendered to the Western Wei forces. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Yuansu soon joined the Western Wei court. He was appointed cavalry general-in-chief, commander unequalled in honor, and palace attendant. He was also granted the noble title of Duke of Anhua 安化 county. During the early Empero
2 CONC r Ming reign (557–559) of the Northern Zhou, he was named Duke of Jicheng 棘城 commandery. In 563, Xiao Yuansu was appointed grand master of the palace. In 565, he was sent out as governor of Xianyang 咸陽 (northeast of modern Xianyan
2 CONC g, Shaanxi) commandery, where he earned a good reputation for his able administration. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 569, Xiao Yuansu was appointed regional inspector of Lingzhou 陵州 (administrative seat, modern Renshou 仁壽, Sichuan). However, before he could take up this position, he was assigned to Xiangyang 襄陽 (modern Xiangyang, Hubei) with 
2 CONC Yuwen Zhi 宇文直 (d. 574), Duke of Weiguo 衛國. In 574, he became junior mentor of the heir designate, Yuwen Yun 宇文贇 (559–580), the future Emperor Xuan (r. 578–579). Xiao Yuansu presented him the “Shao fu zhen” 少傅箴 (A junior mentor's a
2 CONC dmonition). Yuwen Yun was quite pleased with the piece, and he sent Yuansu a letter expressing his appreciation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 577, Xiao Yuansu was appointed regional inspector of Fengzhou 豐州 (administrative seat, modern Yun county 鄖縣, Hubei). When Yuwen Yun acceded to the imperial throne in 578, Xiao Yuansu was assigned as regional inspector of Luozho
2 CONC u 洛州 (administrative seat in modern Yong-nian 永年, Hebei). Ca. 580, he was named general-in-chief. When the Sui replaced the Northern Zhou in 581, Xiao Yuansu was appointed regional inspector of Beizhou 貝州 (administrative seat in m
2 CONC odern Qinghe 清河, Hebei). He declined the appointment on the grounds that he had to care for his aged mother. The emperor granted his request. Xiao Yuansu died in 584 at the age of forty-six. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Yuansu's biography in the ''Zhou shu'' mentions a collection of his writings in ten ''juan''. It also says he compiled the ''Wen hai'' 文海 (A sea of literature), a collection of poems and unrhymed writings of contemporary writ
2 CONC ers in forty ''juan''. It also credits him with the ''Huaihai luanli zhi'' 淮海亂離志 (Record of turmoil and dislocation in the Huai River area) in four ''juan'', which is an account of the Hou Jing rebellion. The monograph on bibliogr
2 CONC aphy of the ''Sui shu'' lists the ''Wen hai'' in fifty ''juan'' without indicating the compiler's name. The two Tang histories record the ''Wen hai'' in thirty-six ''juan'' under Xiao Yuansu's name. This was lost during the Song. 
2 CONC The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists the ''Huaihai luanli zhi'' as a work by Xiao Shiyi 蕭世怡 (d. 568). The two Tang histories list it under Xiao Yuansu's name. This also was lost in the Song. His only extant work 
2 CONC is the “Shao fu zhen,” which is preserved in Xiao Yuansu's ''Zhou shu'' biography.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng 曹道衡 and Fu Gang 傅剛. Xiao Tong pingzhuan 蕭統評傳, 97–102. Nanjing: Nanjing daxue chubanshe, 2001. 
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 393–94.
2 CONT
