1 CONT Daxing Xiao10
1 CONT ????????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. 
1 CONT ——— 
1 CONT 1. In October 690, when he ceded the imperial throne to his mother
1 CONT Empress Wu and was made heir to the throne, his family name was
1 CONT changed into Wu which was the family name of his mother. 
1 CONT 2. His original family name Li, the family name of the emperors of
1 CONT the Tang Dynasty, was restored at a date not indicated by ancient
1 CONT Chinese historical books, perhaps in 705 at the fall of Empress Wu. 
1 CONT 3. This is the original given name of Emperor Ruizong. The literal
1 CONT meaning is "disk of the sun appearing above the horizon
1 CONT at daybreak". 
1 CONT 4. Had his name shortened into Lun ("disk") in December 669. 
1 CONT 5. Had his name changed into Dan (literal meaning: "dawn")
1 CONT in August 683. 
1 CONT 6. Had his name changed back to Lun when he ceded the imperial
1 CONT throne to his mother Empress Wu in October 690 and was made
1 CONT heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 7. Had his name changed back to Dan after he was reduced to a
1 CONT princely rank in 698 when his older brother Zhongzong replaced
1 CONT him as heir to the throne. This name became his taboo name when
1 CONT he was restored as emperor in 710. 
1 CONT 8. Ceded the imperial throne to his mother Empress Wu who
1 CONT became "emperor" while Ruizong was made heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 9. Abdicated in favor of his son Emperor Xuanzong and received
1 CONT the title Taishang Huang (???). In practice, continued to rule
1 CONT through his son until the palace coup of July 29, 713, and officially
1 CONT ceded full power to his son on July 30. 
1 CONT 10. Final version of his posthumous name as given in 754. 
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong (June 22, 662 – July 13, 716), personal name Li Dan, was the fifth and ninth emperor of Tang Dynasty. He was the eighth son of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu.
1 CONT
1 CONT In February 684, his mother Empress Wu demoted his older brother Emperor Zhongzong who had attempted to rule free of his mother, and named him emperor. Ruizong, however, was under control of his mother and didn't have any real pow
1 CONC er. In October 690 he ceded the imperial throne to his mother who was installed as "emperor" (huangdi, ??), the only woman in Chinese history ever to rule as emperor. Ruizong was reduced to the position of "heir to the throne". In
1 CONC  the following years, Empress Wu's family members (from the Wu family) tried to have one of them named heir to the throne, but the empress resisted these calls. Eventually, in October 698, faced with foreign invasion and dissatisf
1 CONC action at home, Empress Wu yielded to officials still loyal to the Li family (the Tang imperial family overthrown by the empress) and so she clearly hinted that she would be succeeded by a member of the Li family by appointing her
1 CONC  son the demoted Emperor Zhongzong as heir to the throne. On that occasion, Ruizong was reduced to a princely rank.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruizong lived the next twelve years away from political intrigues and did not take part in the coup that saw the overthrow of Empress Wu and the restoration of Zhongzong as emperor in 705. The five years of Zhongzong's reign were 
1 CONC dominated by Zhongzong's empress consort, Empress Wei (???), and her lover Wu Sansi (???), the nephew of the deceased Empress Wu, who both dominated the weak emperor. In the beginning of July 710, Emperor Zhongzong died, allegedly
1 CONC  poisoned by Empress Wei who then named Zhongzong's only surviving son, a teenager called Li Chongmao, as emperor. A mere two weeks later, Princess Taiping, the older sister of Ruizong, launched a coup with the help of Ruizong's t
1 CONC hird son, Li Longji (the future Emperor Xuanzong), which resulted in the death of Empress Wei and the overthrow of the teenage emperor. Ruizong was unwillingly restored as emperor on July 25 by his sister and his son. Li Longji, w
1 CONC ho had proved his worth during the coup, was named crown prince with the consent of Ruizong's eldest son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon, however, tensions between Princess Taiping and Li Longji mounted. Princess Taiping, who had many supporters at court, hoped to ascend the imperial throne as her mother Empress Wu had done. Ruizong exiled his sister to the pr
1 CONC ovinces, hoping to defuse tension, but Li Longji, who feared that Princess Taiping would hold him responsible for her exile and would have him murdered, asked his father to recall the princess, who was allowed to return to the imp
1 CONC erial court. Eventually, in September 712, a disgruntled Ruizong, tired of court feuds, abdicated in favor of his son the crown prince Li Longji. Suddenly it was Princess Taiping who was at risk of falling victim to her nephew the
1 CONC  new emperor. The princess, alarmed by Ruizong's decision, managed to have the court name him Taishang Huang (???, sometimes translated as "retired emperor", or "emperor emeritus"), a position in which Ruizong was to keep effectiv
1 CONC e control of power with the new emperor Xuangzong only formally attending court audiences and ceremonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fearing for her future, Princess Taiping launched a coup in the end of July 713 to overthrow Emperor Xuanzong. As she was a woman and could not personally lead troops, the coup failed and all her supporters were killed. Princess T
1 CONC aiping fled to a Buddhist monastery in the mountains. She returned three days later and was allowed to commit suicide at home. On July 30, one day after the coup, Ruizong officially announced he was ceding full power to his son Em
1 CONC peror Xuanzong. Ruizong was allowed to keep the title Taishang Huang but lived away from politics until his death three years later in 716.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Zhongzong Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 684–690 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Empress Wu 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Shang Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 710–712 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Emperor Xuanzong 
0 @N6000000002188099930@ NOTE Princess Taiping
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Taiping (Chinese: ????) (between 651 and 662 – October 1 or 2, 713), was a princess of Tang dynasty of China. She was the daughter of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu Zetian. She had become an important and respected figure
1 CONC  during her mother's reign as Empress under the second Zhou dynasty 690-705.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Taiping was an advocate for the preservation of the Tang dynasty and its imperial Li family. After the death of Empress Wu Zetian in 705, Princess Taiping aligned herself with her nephew Li Longji (later Emperor Xuanzong)
1 CONC  to regain power for the Tang dynasty. In 710, she and Li Longji successfully foiled Empress Wei's plot to gain control of power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Taiping was well-known for her beauty. In the Chinese 'black' history (mishi/??), it has been rumoured that Princess Taiping after failure of two past marriages, had a love affair with her nephew Li Long Ji (Xuan Zong). M
1 CONC ishi says that Xuan Zong actually confessed his love for his aunt in his autobiography. Li Long Ji confessed that Princess Taiping was his love at first sight. He pictured her as the divine serenity and she was all he could dream 
1 CONC of in perfection. The truth still remains hidden however.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Emperor Xuanzong ascended the throne in 712, Princess Taiping's seniority and power was seen as a threat to the young emperor and after a brief struggle, Princess Taiping was given an honorable death sentence to commit suici
1 CONC de by hanging herself in 713.
0 @N6000000002188193876@ NOTE Emperor Wen of Western Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Xi) Wei Wendi ((?)???)
1 CONT Family name: Yuan (?, yuán)
1 CONT Given name: Baoju (??, bao jù)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Xiaowu (?, wén),
1 CONT literary meaning: "civil"
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Wen of Western Wei ((?)???) (507-551), personal name Yuan Baoju (???), was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state to Northern Wei. In 534, Yuan Baoju, then the Prince of Nanyang, fo
1 CONC llowed his cousin Emperor Xiaowu in fleeing from the capital Luoyang to Chang'an, after a fallout between Emperor Xiaowu and the paramount general Gao Huan. However, Emperor Xiaowu's relationship to the general that he then depend
1 CONC ed on, Yuwen Tai, soon deteriorated as well, and around the new year 535, Yuwen Tai poisoned Emperor Xiaowu to death, making Yuan Baoju emperor (as Emperor Wen). As Gao Huan had, late in 534, made Yuan Shanjian the son of Emperor 
1 CONC Wen's cousin Yuan Dan (??) the Prince of Qinghe emperor (as Emperor Xiaojing), thus establishing Eastern Wei, Emperor Wen was known as Western Wei's first emperor, formalizing the division. Emperor Wen's relationship with Yuwen ap
1 CONC peared cordial, but he was unable to exercise much real power.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 Reign
1 CONT     * 3 Era name
1 CONT     * 4 Personal information
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Baoju was born in 507. His father Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Jingzhao was the son of Emperor Xiaowen and a younger brother of the then-reigning Emperor Xuanwu. His mother was recorded as Yuan Yu's concubine Lady Yang. (She mi
1 CONC ght or might not be the same person as the Lady Li referred to below, as some historical records indicate that Lady Li was initially surnamed Yang, and only became known as Lady Li after Yuan Yu, wanting her to be part of a promin
1 CONC ent family, had the aristocratic family Li adopt her.) He had three other brothers, at least one of whom, Yuan Baoyue (???), was older, and born of Ladi Li.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Yu favored Lady Li and had no liking for his wife, Princess Yu -- a sister to Emperor Xuanwu's wife Empress Yu. Empress Yu, because of this, once summoned Lady Li to the palace, beat her severely, and then forced her to becom
1 CONC e a Buddhist nun. Only after the intercession of Empress Yu's father Yu Jing (??) was Lady Li returned to Yuan Yu. Meanwhile, in 508, Yuan Yu himself was punished by Emperor Xuanwu for corruption -- he was caned 50 times and demot
1 CONC ed to the governorship of Ji Province (??, modern central Hebei). In anger, he rebelled at the capital of Ji Province, Xindu (??, in modern Hengshui, Hebei), alleging falsely that Emperor Xuanwu's uncle Gao Zhao had murdered the e
1 CONC mperor and declaring himself emperor. Yuan Yu's rebellion was soon defeated by the general Li Ping (??), and during his being delivered to the capital Luoyang, Gao had him killed. At that time, Lady Li was pregnant, and she was pe
1 CONC rmitted to give birth and then was executed. Emperor Xuanwu did not execute any of Yuan Yu's sons, but had them, including Yuan Baoju, put under arrest at Zongzheng Temple (???). Assuming that Lady Li and Lady Yang were in fact th
1 CONC e same person, this also meant that Yuan Baoju grew up without either parent. He and his brothers remained at Zongzheng Temple and were released only after Emperor Xuanwu's death in 515. During the reign of Emperor Xuanwu's son Em
1 CONC peror Xiaoming, Emperor Xiaoming's mother Empress Dowager Hu posthumously recreated Yuan Yu the Prince of Lintao, and Yuan Baoju and his brothers then observed a mourning period for their parents. Yuan Baoyue inherited the title, 
1 CONC but Yuan Baoju did not possess any titles at the moment, although he was made a general. Despite Empress Dowager Hu's rehabiilitation of Yuan Yu, however, Yuan Baoju was not impressed at her toleration of corruption, particularly 
1 CONC by her lovers, and he secretly plotted with Emperor Xiaoming to have her lovers killed. When this plot was discovered, he was stripped of the office he held. In 525, he married his wife Lady Yifu, the daughter of a moderately prom
1 CONC inent aristocratic family. (In his youth, Yuan Baoju was described by the Book of Wei as frivolous, alcoholic, and sexually immoral, but this description is highly suspect in that the Book of Wei was written by Wei Shou, an offici
1 CONC al of Eastern Wei, the rival of Western Wei, for which Yuan Baoju would eventually become emperor.) In 528, Emperor Xiaoming created him the Marquess of Shao County, and in 530, Emperor Xiaozhuang created him the Prince of Nanyang
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In 532, after several years of civil war, the victorious general Gao Huan made Yuan Baoju's cousin Yuan Xiu the Prince of Pingyang emperor (as Emperor Xiaowu). Emperor Xiaowu was not happy about Gao's hold on the military, and he 
1 CONC entered into alliances with the independent generals Yuwen Tai and Heba Sheng (???), seeking to resist Gao's control. Yuan Baoju served in Emperor Xiaowu's administration as a general. In 534, Emperor Xiaowu planned to act against
1 CONC  Gao, but Gao discovered his plan and instead marched on Luoyang. Emperor Xiaowu decided to flee to Yuwen's territory, and Yuan Baoju accompanied Emperor Xiaowu in doing so, arriving at Chang'an in late 534. Also accompanying Empe
1 CONC ror Xiaowu was Yuan Baoju's sister Yuan Mingyue (???) -- who was in an incestuous relationship with Emperor Xiaowu. Yuwen did not tolerate Emperor Xiaowu's incestuous relationships with Yuan Mingyue and two other cousins, and even
1 CONC tually he had Yuan Mingyue killed. Emperor Xiaowu became angry, and his relationship with Yuwen deteriorated. Around the new year 535, Yuwen poisoned him to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Initially, Yuwen was poised to make Emperor Xiaowu's nephew Yuan Zan (??) the Prince of Guangping the new emperor. However, under suggestion of Yuan Shun (??) the Prince of Puyang, who argued that Yuan Zan was too young, Yuwen cha
1 CONC nged his mind and made Yuan Baoju, then 27, emperor instead (as Emperor Wen). As Gao had earlier declared Yuan Shanjian, the son of Yuan Baoju's cousin Yuan Dan (??) the Prince of Qinghe, emperor, Gao's territory became known as E
1 CONC astern Wei, with Yuan Shanjian (Emperor Xiaojing) as emperor, and Yuwen's territory became known as Western Wei, with Emperor Wen as emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuwen Tai publicly deferred to Emperor Wen on most matters, but Yuwen held actual power, with Emperor Wen not being able to exercise much independent authority. Throughout the early years of his reign, there were serious doubts as
1 CONC  to whether Western Wei would survive, as Eastern Wei was then the much stronger state, and Gao Huan made repeated attempts to conquer Western Wei. However, with Yuwen and other generals capably defending the territory, Western We
1 CONC i was able to withstand Gao's assaults.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 535, Emperor Wen posthumously honored his father Yuan Yu as Emperor Wenjing, and he posthumously honored his mother Lady Yang as empress. He created his wife Princess Yifu empress, and her son Yuan Qin crown prince. His marriag
1 CONC e with Empress Yifu was said to be a happy one, as she was virtuous and beautiful, and Emperor Wen respected her greatly. She bore him 12 children, although only Yuan Qin and Yuan Wu (??) the Prince of Wudu survived infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 538, with Western Wei under the threat of attack by Rouran, Yuwen first tried to alleviate the situation by marrying a daughter of a member of the imperial clan to Yujiulü Tahan (?????), the brother of Rouran's Chiliantoubingdo
1 CONC ufa Khan Yujiulü Anagui, but then, believing that to be insufficient, he asked Emperor Wen to divorce Empress Yifu and marry Yujiulü Anagui's daughter. Emperor Wen agreed, and divorced Empress Yifu, making her a Buddhist nun. He t
1 CONC hen married Yujiulü Anagui's daughter and created her empress. For a while, this brought peace with Rouran.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later in 538, with Western Wei then (temporariily) controlling the old Northern Wei capital Luoyang, but with Luoyang under attack, Emperor Wen (who had wanted to visit the imperial ancestral tombs in Luoyang) and Yuwen led troops
1 CONC  to reinforce Luoyang's defenses, leaving the official Zhou Huida (???) and Crown Prince Qin in Chang'an. However, with the forces engaged in battle, Emperor Wen eventually became stuck at Hengnong (??, in modern Sanmenxia, Henan)
1 CONC , when Chang'an was taken by rebelling former Eastern Wei troops who had been taken captive previously by Western Wei, forcing Zhou and Crown Prince Qin to flee as well. Yuwen was eventually able to disengage after abandoning Luoy
1 CONC ang, and he put down the rebellion, allowing Emperor Wen to return to Chang'an.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although the former Empress Yifu had been deposed and made a Buddhist nun, Empress Yujiulü was still not happy about her presence in the capital. in 540, Emperor Wen therefore made Yuan Wu the governor of Qin Province (??, roughly
1 CONC  modern Tianshui, Gansu), and had Empress Yifu accompany Yuan Wu to Qin Province. However, because he still hoped to welcome her back to the palace one day, he secretly told her to keep her hair uncut, rather than shaved like a Bu
1 CONC ddhist nun. At this time, however, Rouran made a major attack on Western Wei, and many officials thought that the attack was on behalf of Empress Yujiulü. Emperor Wen felt compelled to order Empress Yifu to commit suicide, and he 
1 CONC did. Soon thereafter, Empress Yujiulü, who was pregnant, died during childbirth.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 548, Yuwen and Yuan Qin were on a grand tour of the provinces when Emperor Wen grew ill, and when they heard the news, Yuwen returned to Chang'an quickly, although by the time they returned, Emperor Wen had recovered.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 549, Emperor Wen issued an edict -- probably as Yuwen requested -- ordering that the names of the ethnic Xianbei, changed to Han names during the reign of Emperor Xiaowen, be changed back to the original Xianbei names.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 550, Gao Huan's son Gao Yang forced Eastern Wei's Emperor Xiaojing to yield the throne to him, ending Eastern Wei and starting Northern Qi (as its Emperor Wenxuan). Emperor Wen therefore became the only claimant to the Northern
1 CONC  Wei throne. Yuwen, declaring Northern Qi a rebel state, launched a major attack, but Gao Yang himself commanded a large army to defend against the attack, and Western Wei not only did not make gains, but lost a number of province
1 CONC s to Northern Qi.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 551, Emperor Wen died and was buried with honors due an emperor, with Empress Yujiulü, although eventually Empress Yifu was buried with him. (It is not clear whether she displaced Empress Yujiulü or not.) Yuan Qin succeeded him
1 CONC  (as Emperor Fei).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Era name
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Datong (?? dà tong) 535-551
1 CONT
